All right, let's get to it.

43
00:03:13,280 --> 00:03:14,320
API.

44
00:03:14,670 --> 00:03:17,360
Those three little letters generate quite the buzz, don't they?

45
00:03:17,600 --> 00:03:24,356
First, I'd like to take some time up front here and clearly break down what is meant by application programming interface.

46
00:03:24,718 --> 00:03:32,880
By understanding its purpose and a touch of its history, I think you'll start to see its importance and begin to understand why this term keeps popping up in your life.

47
00:03:33,134 --> 00:03:39,200
After we get a good grasp on the what and the why of APIs, we'll go and learn how to explore two existing ones.

48
00:03:39,280 --> 00:03:43,812
We'll get familiar with several handy tools that you can use to interact with web-based APIs.

49
00:03:44,718 --> 00:03:52,452
We'll use those tools to hit an external web API to send actual text messages, and we'll get some interesting data back from a streaming music service API.

50
00:03:52,718 --> 00:03:58,080
Once we get some practice using the APIs, we'll use them in a web application that you'll actually publish.

51
00:03:58,350 --> 00:04:07,588
The application that we're gonna be building is going to receive user-submitted photos via a text message, and then we'll use an API to display a collection of all these photos.

52
00:04:07,950 --> 00:04:09,636
And you get to come up with the prompt.

53
00:04:10,030 --> 00:04:12,000
Send me the latest photo on your phone.

54
00:04:12,430 --> 00:04:13,508
Dog pics, please.

55
00:04:13,998 --> 00:04:14,640
What's for dinner?

56
00:04:15,040 --> 00:04:15,844
Whatever you want.

57
00:04:16,174 --> 00:04:19,620
I can't wait to see what you build and what your family and friends submit to it.

58
00:04:20,174 --> 00:04:24,004
We're going to be strictly using APIs in this course, we won't be writing our own.

59
00:04:24,320 --> 00:04:29,840
While you most likely are gonna end up learning some best practices in API design, we won't be covering that specifically.

60
00:04:30,160 --> 00:04:35,908
There are many great courses available that show the creation thought process, and I've linked to some of my faves in the notes attached to this video.

61
00:04:36,878 --> 00:04:42,288
In my opinion, the best way to start learning about any acronym is to focus on each individual letter.

62
00:04:42,510 --> 00:04:46,948
For A, P, I, I think we should start on that last character, the I, interface.

63
00:04:47,248 --> 00:04:50,180
Let's talk a bit about the interfaces that you've seen already.

64
00:04:51,342 --> 00:04:53,060
Interfaces are all around us.

65
00:04:53,328 --> 00:04:56,768
For instance, this radio here, it has a familiar interface.

66
00:04:56,928 --> 00:05:02,500
The interface allows me to do things like change the volume, change the station, I can turn it off and on.

67
00:05:02,848 --> 00:05:06,448
I literally have no idea how anything inside this thing works.

68
00:05:06,670 --> 00:05:11,108
But still, the interface allows me to control the way that it works through the options that are exposed.

69
00:05:11,726 --> 00:05:12,996
And that's pretty great, isn't it?

70
00:05:13,262 --> 00:05:16,368
The magic that is happening is completely abstracted away from me.

71
00:05:16,448 --> 00:05:20,420
I'm still in control of what has been determined that I, a user, can handle.

72
00:05:21,102 --> 00:05:23,328
Interfaces usually evolve over time.

73
00:05:23,630 --> 00:05:25,648
Like an alarm clock radio, for example.

74
00:05:25,808 --> 00:05:32,768
It has my familiar volume and station controls, and it also adds some new features, like changing the time and setting the alarm.

75
00:05:32,928 --> 00:05:38,928
I still have no idea how this works implementation-wise, but the interface is something that I can understand.

76
00:05:39,728 --> 00:05:40,516
Usually.

77
00:05:41,390 --> 00:05:52,804
And as technology evolves and new options come into existence, they make their way into the new versions of the interface, still abstracting away things for us, but enabling us to be in control of the new features.

78
00:05:53,262 --> 00:05:58,052
Sometimes these physical interfaces get so recognizable they even make it into GUIs.

79
00:05:58,670 --> 00:05:59,408
Hey, did you hear that?

80
00:05:59,598 --> 00:06:00,208
There it is again.

81
00:06:00,430 --> 00:06:03,940
GUI, G-U-I, graphical user interface.

82
00:06:04,558 --> 00:06:06,768
This is my streaming music app on my phone.

83
00:06:07,022 --> 00:06:10,288
There's my familiar play button in the user interface, or UI.

84
00:06:11,008 --> 00:06:16,612
And I know what the play button does when I press it, but I have no idea how it does it.

85
00:06:16,782 --> 00:06:18,848
And I'm okay with that level of abstraction.

86
00:06:19,088 --> 00:06:21,088
What would I do in public transit without music?

87
00:06:21,248 --> 00:06:22,448
I talk to people.

88
00:06:22,688 --> 00:06:24,288
Hi, how's it going?

89
00:06:26,528 --> 00:06:31,968
Now, the developer who wrote this streaming music application, of course, understands how the play button works.

90
00:06:32,128 --> 00:06:35,716
She coded the button so that when it gets clicked, the music starts playing.

91
00:06:36,398 --> 00:06:42,948
Here's the thing though, our developer is also working with quite a few interfaces that are abstracting away some of the details for her.

92
00:06:43,310 --> 00:06:46,532
For instance, she wrote code that happens when the button is clicked.

93
00:06:46,958 --> 00:06:50,660
She probably did that using an event handler on the button, like on click.

94
00:06:51,406 --> 00:06:56,516
What she didn't have to do, however, was write the code that makes the button interact and change with the press.

95
00:06:56,910 --> 00:06:59,588
She didn't have to write the code that makes that familiar clicking noise.

96
00:07:00,014 --> 00:07:01,188
It was already there for her.

97
00:07:01,742 --> 00:07:04,164
Buttons are in fact an interface.

98
00:07:04,622 --> 00:07:10,820
They provide the developer the means of controlling its interactions while abstracting away how that actually works implementation-wise.

99
00:07:11,374 --> 00:07:19,968
This button is part of the API, or application programming interface, and it's available from the application framework that was used to create the app.

100
00:07:20,270 --> 00:07:27,236
Because most apps need buttons, this interface has been created and provided to our developer, or application programmer, to use.

101
00:07:27,726 --> 00:07:33,380
By implementing the button interface, our developer was able to make her code run when the button was clicked.

102
00:07:33,710 --> 00:07:42,148
It's worth noting that the code probably also uses a media player API that is provided with the operating system of the device, like Android or iOS.

103
00:07:42,798 --> 00:07:48,868
This media player API abstracts away needing to understand details of how to send audio data to hardware.

104
00:07:49,390 --> 00:07:53,668
The developer knows they just want to call the play method on the media player API.

105
00:07:54,288 --> 00:07:59,172
I suppose too, the song isn't stored on the device, it's being streamed over the network.

106
00:07:59,408 --> 00:08:03,492
So a web-based API call has been made over the internet to get that data.

107
00:08:03,950 --> 00:08:09,636
It's actually APIs all the way down to the ones and zeros, providing abstractions at many levels.

108
00:08:10,286 --> 00:08:13,124
So let's take a look at what all of these interfaces have in common.

109
00:08:13,614 --> 00:08:20,368
They all define ways for us to interact or communicate with an object, whether that object be physical or software.

110
00:08:20,686 --> 00:08:24,292
And as a user of the interface, we don't need to understand the implementation.

111
00:08:24,528 --> 00:08:25,988
We don't need to know how it works.

112
00:08:26,368 --> 00:08:29,956
We just need to know what we've been allowed to change or see.

113
00:08:30,382 --> 00:08:33,220
Interfaces abstract away the implementation.

114
00:08:33,774 --> 00:08:42,212
Now, while the UI or user interface is made for the user of the application, the API is made for the application programmer to use and extend in their applications.

115
00:08:42,926 --> 00:08:49,316
With the I covered in our API, let's take a deeper look at the rest of the API acronym.

116
00:08:50,350 --> 00:08:52,260
An API is a contract of sorts.

117
00:08:52,528 --> 00:08:57,892
It defines how it is expected to be used and it defines what you can expect to receive by using it.

118
00:08:58,542 --> 00:09:02,852
Think of it as a set of tools designed for software developers like yourself to use.

119
00:09:03,214 --> 00:09:10,148
An API is created to help make your life easier by giving you access to data and by providing you with an abstraction of the implementation.

120
00:09:10,638 --> 00:09:13,476
You know, not needing to know how it's doing whatever it's doing.

121
00:09:14,350 --> 00:09:17,264
They save you from needing to create everything yourself.

122
00:09:17,454 --> 00:09:21,744
Like when designed well, they make nearly impossible things happen with just a few lines of code.

123
00:09:21,966 --> 00:09:25,316
Now, you're probably starting to see why it's important to get familiar with them, right?

124
00:09:25,678 --> 00:09:30,784
I want to do a quick word of warning here about some naming issues that you'll probably run into.

125
00:09:31,024 --> 00:09:36,708
These days, the term API is almost always used as a way to explain web-based APIs.

126
00:09:36,944 --> 00:09:43,424
In fact, it's probably safe to assume that when you hear API in the wild, it's most likely that people are talking about a web-based API.

127
00:09:43,598 --> 00:09:44,900
We'll explore those here in a few.

128
00:09:45,646 --> 00:09:53,184
However, if we focus on all types of APIs, instead of just the web-based subset, we'll get a better look into why web-based APIs are so popular.

129
00:09:53,358 --> 00:09:54,660
So hang tight, we're getting there.

130
00:09:55,024 --> 00:09:59,024
APIs are everywhere in your software journey, just about everywhere you look.

131
00:09:59,310 --> 00:10:03,044
Your programming language, whichever one that might be, provides many APIs.

132
00:10:03,278 --> 00:10:06,084
Let's take a look at one that is common in all of them.

133
00:10:06,414 --> 00:10:10,624
They all provide some way of working with strings, you know, like a series of characters.

134
00:10:11,022 --> 00:10:12,516
Wanna make your text all uppercase?

135
00:10:12,782 --> 00:10:15,184
Just call the API method, and voila.

136
00:10:15,726 --> 00:10:23,172
You could probably create the uppercase string all by yourself by doing some sort of low-level bit math on each character, but you don't need to.

137
00:10:23,566 --> 00:10:28,388
Because your programming language has abstracted away that for you and provided a handy string API.

138
00:10:28,974 --> 00:10:32,420
It exists because this is a common enough problem that developers run into.

139
00:10:33,166 --> 00:10:42,852
Because these programming language specifications are just interfaces, different operating systems can supply their own implementation so that the code works properly in their environment.

140
00:10:43,406 --> 00:10:49,956
I think an easy way of picturing this is to think about how different operating systems like Windows or Mac OS maintain their files.

141
00:10:50,286 --> 00:10:51,524
They're totally different.

142
00:10:51,886 --> 00:10:55,876
Yet your programming language provides a single API for working with the file system.

143
00:10:56,270 --> 00:11:00,624
You can write one script and it works both in Mac and Windows.

144
00:11:00,814 --> 00:11:03,664
It's the implementation that takes care of all of the details for you.

145
00:11:03,854 --> 00:11:04,676
Pretty cool, right?

146
00:11:05,102 --> 00:11:06,784
Definitely something to be thankful for.

147
00:11:06,990 --> 00:11:11,428
It makes our lives so much easier not having to worry about what operating system is being used.

148
00:11:11,918 --> 00:11:15,424
And the importance of an API to a language cannot be understated.

149
00:11:15,758 --> 00:11:23,364
There have been some fascinating legal cases where it's been questioned if the language API itself, again, not the implementation, can be copyrighted.

150
00:11:23,598 --> 00:11:24,932
Check out the notes for more on that.

151
00:11:25,344 --> 00:11:32,132
Along similar lines, there are different web browsers, you know, like Google Chrome, Microsoft Edge, Safari, Firefox, Brave.

152
00:11:32,366 --> 00:11:38,820
Now, you might not have pondered this before, but isn't it amazing that you can write code for the web and it will work in any of them?

153
00:11:39,118 --> 00:11:45,956
This works because there's a set of web APIs that web browsers all must implement in order to make that magic happen for you.

154
00:11:46,478 --> 00:11:50,916
We're standing on the shoulders of giants, folks, and we really should thank those giants more often.

155
00:11:51,104 --> 00:11:57,284
There are authors and maintainers of libraries that package up code to solve common problems so that you don't need to do it yourself.

156
00:11:57,806 --> 00:12:01,700
For instance, do you know how to programmatically make a photo black and white?

157
00:12:02,222 --> 00:12:02,784
Me neither.

158
00:12:02,958 --> 00:12:07,664
But I can download and import an image package into my application, and suddenly I can.

159
00:12:07,854 --> 00:12:09,412
I just call a method and boom.

160
00:12:10,222 --> 00:12:11,904
I wish real life skills worked this way.

161
00:12:12,110 --> 00:12:14,148
I'd import dancing so it could be more fun at weddings.

162
00:12:14,638 --> 00:12:15,344
Cha-cha-cha!

163
00:12:15,982 --> 00:12:20,452
Frameworks provide an API that allow you to extend what has been provided to make it your own.

164
00:12:20,624 --> 00:12:25,988
Like, for example, most web frameworks provide you with the ability to add a new route to your web application.

165
00:12:26,510 --> 00:12:29,796
It allows you to write out dynamic data to a template that you specify.

166
00:12:30,254 --> 00:12:33,188
And you do all of this with just a handful of lines of code.

167
00:12:33,646 --> 00:12:40,548
You don't need to understand how it's doing things, but you do need to know how to write an implementation to what the framework is expecting.

168
00:12:40,878 --> 00:12:46,780
In this case, you're using the API, the contract, to write a valid implementation that meets the requirement.

169
00:12:47,214 --> 00:12:50,532
Now, all these APIs that we just talked about, they're local to our code base.

170
00:12:50,704 --> 00:12:52,064
They exist within our code.

171
00:12:52,304 --> 00:12:54,564
We use them to avoid recreating the wheel.

172
00:12:54,864 --> 00:13:03,940
That is to say, they assist us in accomplishing tasks that we need to perform by abstracting away a ton of the work for us, the stuff that we developers shouldn't be worrying about.

173
00:13:04,174 --> 00:13:06,180
We should be focusing on the business problem at hand.

174
00:13:06,446 --> 00:13:11,524
And that means a big part of your job is figuring out how to leverage these APIs to your advantage.

175
00:13:12,046 --> 00:13:15,504
Now, I think this is an excellent time for you to take a break.

176
00:13:15,758 --> 00:13:19,556
I just gave you a lot to think about, and I'd like you to take some time and breathe.

177
00:13:20,622 --> 00:13:23,088
Why don't you jot down some APIs that you've used today?

178
00:13:23,248 --> 00:13:26,128
Even if you weren't coding, I know that you've seen some in action.

179
00:13:26,382 --> 00:13:30,308
If you're having a hard time thinking of some, I'll give you a hint.

180
00:13:30,894 --> 00:13:34,288
You're watching a video right now inside of a web browser.

181
00:13:35,342 --> 00:13:44,128
When we return, we'll explore the concept of remote APIs, ones that aren't executing locally in our code base, and why that is such a powerful concept.

182
00:13:59,950 --> 00:14:01,888
It took me a while to find this remote control.

183
00:14:02,318 --> 00:14:03,088
It was in the couch.

184
00:14:03,438 --> 00:14:04,452
It's always on the couch.

185
00:14:04,768 --> 00:14:07,568
Anyways, this remote has an interface on it, right?

186
00:14:07,648 --> 00:14:08,548
There's a power button.

187
00:14:08,878 --> 00:14:11,328
And when I press the channel button, it changes the channel.

188
00:14:11,488 --> 00:14:19,396
When I press the volume button, it sends the request to my radio, and the volume changes as if I turn the volume up using the interface on the device itself.

189
00:14:19,918 --> 00:14:21,348
And we know why that's important.

190
00:14:21,646 --> 00:14:23,408
I want to remotely control my device.

191
00:14:23,568 --> 00:14:25,380
I don't want to get up from my chair.

192
00:14:25,742 --> 00:14:30,212
There are, of course, more less lazy reasons as to why we'd want to remotely control objects.

193
00:14:31,118 --> 00:14:33,648
This cute little robot, for instance, has a remote API.

194
00:14:33,808 --> 00:14:37,956
You can send commands to it and it responds, and it even sends back sensor information.

195
00:14:38,286 --> 00:14:41,092
These traffic signs can change based on an API call.

196
00:14:41,678 --> 00:14:44,448
This drone here is remotely controlled by an app on my phone.

197
00:14:44,688 --> 00:14:45,008
See?

198
00:14:48,688 --> 00:14:53,220
And it's not just physical objects that benefit from remote APIs.

199
00:14:53,518 --> 00:14:57,328
Oftentimes we don't have space on our local machines for all the data that's available.

200
00:14:57,518 --> 00:15:01,860
Like, for example, have you ever used one of those apps that helps you figure out what song is playing?

201
00:15:02,222 --> 00:15:08,644
You send up a snippet of a song that you heard playing at a grocery store using your microphone, and it sends back exactly what song it is.

202
00:15:09,198 --> 00:15:10,244
It's usually Phil Collins.

203
00:15:10,368 --> 00:15:13,252
It turns out that both grocery stores and yours truly love Phil Collins.

204
00:15:14,158 --> 00:15:19,780
You don't have room for all the songs in the world on your phone, and with this remote API, you don't need to have all of them.

205
00:15:20,174 --> 00:15:24,068
It does all the searching and processing, and it's surprisingly quick.

206
00:15:24,590 --> 00:15:26,308
How can it possibly do that?

207
00:15:26,926 --> 00:15:28,848
I don't know, but I love the interface.

208
00:15:28,928 --> 00:15:29,648
It's so fast.

209
00:15:29,808 --> 00:15:33,668
Ooh, that's another benefit of remote APIs: computational power.

210
00:15:34,446 --> 00:15:40,768
Because an API removes the limitations of your local device, you can gain access to seemingly infinite amounts of power.

211
00:15:41,008 --> 00:15:43,908
A good example of this is the AR app of Google Translate.

212
00:15:44,558 --> 00:15:51,568
You can use the camera on your phone, and in near real time, it's able to translate directly to your screen, like instantaneously.

213
00:15:51,854 --> 00:15:57,860
It's using a ton of processing power to make that magic happen, and that processing is happening elsewhere, in La Nube.

214
00:15:58,766 --> 00:16:07,488
It took quite a bit of experimentation and some evolution of ideas, but over time, we finally turned this remote API dream into a reality.

215
00:16:07,854 --> 00:16:15,888
Now I'm gonna drop some history into the teacher's notes, but I just want you to know that the struggle to achieve this concept of remote APIs was very real.

216
00:16:16,270 --> 00:16:19,968
Now, most of the problem was that there wasn't a standard that everyone loved.

217
00:16:20,174 --> 00:16:23,076
Things were pretty clunky for a while as these ideas were sussed out.

218
00:16:23,568 --> 00:16:25,316
But then it happened.

219
00:16:25,648 --> 00:16:35,364
A new architectural style appeared that was clear and allowed us developers to interact with specific resources over the web, and it quite literally changed everything.

220
00:16:35,918 --> 00:16:42,628
This style rapidly emerged as a winner, and just about everyone began offering and consuming external APIs over the internet.

221
00:16:43,278 --> 00:16:46,916
Now, what I'm talking about here is REST, or representational state transfer.

222
00:16:47,182 --> 00:16:51,160
It was the approach that we needed, and honestly, it's the abstraction that we developers deserve.

223
00:16:51,662 --> 00:16:58,768
Like we talked about already, the popularity of REST spread so rapidly that it nearly completely overtook the term API.

224
00:16:59,022 --> 00:17:05,028
It's like how we use the word Xerox to mean photocopy, even though Xerox is just a brand of photocopier.

225
00:17:05,326 --> 00:17:08,368
Xerox does other stuff too, I think.

226
00:17:09,230 --> 00:17:10,596
That's how pervasive REST is.

227
00:17:11,214 --> 00:17:14,688
REST is for sure not the end all be all of remote APIs.

228
00:17:14,848 --> 00:17:18,208
There are definitely some limitations, which we'll discuss here in a bit.

229
00:17:18,580 --> 00:17:24,196
Technology is ever evolving, and of course there's movements that are still advancing the concept of the best remote APIs.

230
00:17:24,622 --> 00:17:27,008
Some terms you might even have heard, like GraphQL.

