00:18:18,768 --> 00:18:21,808
It might have been a while since you thought about how the web works.

240
00:18:21,968 --> 00:18:28,964
There are a smattering of terms that I'd like to reload into your working memory before we talk more about how REST uses them.

241
00:18:29,328 --> 00:18:30,820
So let's use the web.

242
00:18:31,438 --> 00:18:34,372
I've got my computer here, and on it is an internet browser.

243
00:18:34,702 --> 00:18:39,428
The browser is a web client, and I'm going to use it to connect to a server.

244
00:18:39,886 --> 00:18:46,948
I'm going to do this by putting a location in the address bar, a universal resource locator, or URL to be exact.

245
00:18:47,598 --> 00:18:53,284
You might also hear this referred to by its superset term, URI, universal resource identifier.

246
00:18:54,158 --> 00:18:59,652
Now our URL here has a scheme portion, and this one happens to be HTTP.

247
00:19:00,110 --> 00:19:03,268
HTTP stands for Hypertext Transfer Protocol.

248
00:19:03,598 --> 00:19:05,008
Let's break that down a bit.

249
00:19:05,326 --> 00:19:07,088
Let's grab that last part, protocol.

250
00:19:07,758 --> 00:19:10,608
A protocol is also often likened to a contract.

251
00:19:10,768 --> 00:19:13,636
It's a little lower level than an API, but it's similar in concept.

252
00:19:14,126 --> 00:19:18,128
You can think of it as defining the expectations of how to communicate.

253
00:19:18,288 --> 00:19:28,928
Like, an example that I think really sticks with me is if you've ever been to a rally and someone does one of those chants where they're like, when I say foo, you say bar, foo, bar, one of those.

254
00:19:29,102 --> 00:19:29,924
Here, let's do one.

255
00:19:30,190 --> 00:19:34,288
When I say HTT, you say P, H T T, H T T.

256
00:19:34,862 --> 00:19:38,288
Oh, I just realized I might've made you yell P out loud.

257
00:19:38,606 --> 00:19:39,748
Hope you aren't in a coffee shop.

258
00:19:39,968 --> 00:19:40,580
Awkward.

259
00:19:41,328 --> 00:19:45,008
Anyways, we, you and I, just set up a real quick protocol.

260
00:19:45,198 --> 00:19:53,088
I told you how to respond to my specific request, and you would have been breaking our agreed-upon protocol if you yelled anything else but P.

261
00:19:53,774 --> 00:19:56,768
So the browser creates an HTTP request for you.

262
00:19:56,974 --> 00:20:01,568
Along with the URI, it also specifies a particular HTTP verb.

263
00:20:01,774 --> 00:20:08,048
In this case, it is using the GET verb, which is used to clarify that this request will only receive data.

264
00:20:08,208 --> 00:20:10,116
That is, it won't be making any changes.

265
00:20:10,448 --> 00:20:14,128
The server receives the request that was transferred from the client.

266
00:20:14,288 --> 00:20:15,364
There's that second T.

267
00:20:15,822 --> 00:20:18,048
The server then does the work that it needs to do.

268
00:20:18,318 --> 00:20:22,308
Finally, it generates the resulting web page and sends back a response to the client.

269
00:20:22,670 --> 00:20:23,844
My browser in this case.

270
00:20:24,270 --> 00:20:26,928
The most important part of the response is the body.

271
00:20:27,118 --> 00:20:34,500
For a web page, that contains the HTML, hypertext markup language, which gives us the remaining parts, the HT of our acronym.

272
00:20:34,766 --> 00:20:37,220
Hypertext is text that can link to other text.

273
00:20:37,486 --> 00:20:40,132
In HTML, we perform that linking with anchor tags.

274
00:20:40,718 --> 00:20:43,488
So with that response, the browser renders the page.

275
00:20:43,726 --> 00:20:50,768
When one of those links are clicked, they kick off a brand new HTTP get request, and the process repeats itself over and over.

276
00:20:51,342 --> 00:20:53,968
In between those clicks, though, the protocol's done.

277
00:20:54,158 --> 00:20:56,528
We've made the request, we got the response.

278
00:20:56,974 --> 00:21:00,420
HTTP as it was originally designed is a stateless protocol.

279
00:21:00,782 --> 00:21:03,748
Once that request receives a response, it's all done.

280
00:21:04,174 --> 00:21:10,212
If you want to keep some sort of state, your client will have to manage that itself and send it up with each new request.

281
00:21:10,606 --> 00:21:12,068
Check out the notes for more on this.

282
00:21:13,198 --> 00:21:17,328
There are more HTTP verbs, also known as request methods, besides get.

283
00:21:17,808 --> 00:21:22,288
Most common scenario you see is when you encounter a form that is used to take information from you.

284
00:21:22,478 --> 00:21:28,928
Typically you'll see the verb post used to explain that you are submitting or posting data to the server.

285
00:21:29,168 --> 00:21:36,004
Okay, so our protocol that's used to transfer this hypertext is admittedly a little more complex than our rally chant was.

286
00:21:36,334 --> 00:21:41,028
There's quite a bit of important information in there that is included in the request and the response.

287
00:21:41,454 --> 00:21:45,764
You've probably seen query string parameters in URLs and you know the stuff after the question mark.

288
00:21:46,048 --> 00:21:48,452
This is one way to pass information about the request.

289
00:21:48,942 --> 00:21:54,276
Another common way this information is passed around is through specific key and value pairs called headers.

290
00:21:54,528 --> 00:22:02,532
Both requests and responses have headers, and they're used to help further communicate what is wanted, as well as what has been sent back.

291
00:22:02,928 --> 00:22:09,956
Some of the popular request headers allow you to specify information about the content that you expect back, like the language or the type of content.

292
00:22:10,382 --> 00:22:14,368
You can also state that you want the page if it has changed since a certain time.

293
00:22:14,542 --> 00:22:17,728
This technique allows for caching, which is a powerful concept.

294
00:22:17,838 --> 00:22:20,452
Why get the whole page if you already have it in your local cache?

295
00:22:21,326 --> 00:22:24,688
You can embed authentication information in each request using a header.

296
00:22:24,768 --> 00:22:28,260
And this way, you're essentially keeping your client logged in across requests.

297
00:22:28,878 --> 00:22:30,724
This response also contains headers.

298
00:22:30,928 --> 00:22:33,248
A super important one is the status code.

299
00:22:33,422 --> 00:22:37,924
This lets you know what happened on the server side and can convey things like missing documents.

300
00:22:38,318 --> 00:22:45,092
I'm sure you've seen the old 404 or maybe the page has moved, but hopefully everything is A200 okay.

301
00:22:45,678 --> 00:22:50,564
It can also explain the content type, how large the data returned is, and many more things.

302
00:22:51,630 --> 00:22:52,912
Okay, got it?

303
00:22:53,326 --> 00:22:55,908
Are you feeling good about that little whirlwind tour?

304
00:22:56,192 --> 00:22:59,472
Remember, feel free to make me repeat myself if that was too much.

305
00:22:59,632 --> 00:23:00,452
I totally get it.

