347
00:25:38,254 --> 00:25:40,420
Each of these books is a resource.

348
00:25:40,750 --> 00:25:46,948
We click in to take a closer look at that book resource and we see a link to the author, which is also a resource.

349
00:25:47,214 --> 00:25:51,332
We click into the author resource and we see a collection of all the other books they've written.

350
00:25:51,790 --> 00:25:57,232
Maybe we even see a group of fans, again, another collection, and each of those fans are a resource.

351
00:25:57,550 --> 00:26:01,156
Clicking into a fan resource, we see a collection of their favorite authors and books.

352
00:26:01,712 --> 00:26:03,620
Everything is a resource.

353
00:26:04,878 --> 00:26:05,796
Let's change gears.

354
00:26:06,158 --> 00:26:08,644
How about an image sharing application on your phone?

355
00:26:08,974 --> 00:26:15,652
So we have user resources, and those users post photos, which are resources, and those photos can be loved.

356
00:26:15,886 --> 00:26:17,632
So there's a lover resource.

357
00:26:17,934 --> 00:26:19,472
Maybe there's an album that could be created.

358
00:26:19,552 --> 00:26:24,964
There's an album resource that contains photos, and albums have a collection of contributor resources.

359
00:26:25,486 --> 00:26:30,852
Turns out almost everything we ever want to build can be expressed in terms of resources and collections.

360
00:26:31,822 --> 00:26:42,884
Most of what we want our applications to be able to do to the resources can be expressed through the unfortunate sounding acronym CRUD, and that stands for creating, reading, updating, and deleting.

361
00:26:43,152 --> 00:26:47,204
Now, as a user of the ebook site, all we did was a bunch of reading.

362
00:26:48,366 --> 00:26:49,380
No pun intended.

363
00:26:50,446 --> 00:26:52,240
I actually did intend that one.

