00:26:52,654 --> 00:26:54,052
We were reading, right?

365
00:26:54,560 --> 00:26:56,400
Just getting information to display it.

366
00:26:56,560 --> 00:26:58,640
Like, we retrieved the author page.

367
00:26:58,720 --> 00:27:00,068
We made a read request.

368
00:27:00,558 --> 00:27:04,640
The web browser used a GET request to the specific author page.

369
00:27:04,942 --> 00:27:07,268
The RESTful API follows the same logic.

370
00:27:07,790 --> 00:27:10,500
Your program sends a GET request to a URI.

371
00:27:11,054 --> 00:27:15,812
Then the server responds with data and of course all the HTTP headers you've come to know and love.

372
00:27:16,430 --> 00:27:21,476
The body is typically represented these days as JSON or JavaScript object notation.

373
00:27:22,030 --> 00:27:25,440
JSON provides a great way to structure and nest your data.

374
00:27:25,680 --> 00:27:31,844
Every programming language that I can think of provides some means of turning a JSON string into a native object.

375
00:27:32,718 --> 00:27:37,680
The content type is typically something that you can specify in your request if JSON isn't your thing.

376
00:27:37,902 --> 00:27:40,960
If you recall, there are other HTTP verbs available.

377
00:27:41,166 --> 00:27:45,668
These verbs are used in REST APIs to clearly state the intention of your request.

378
00:27:45,998 --> 00:27:46,960
Want to add an author?

379
00:27:47,246 --> 00:27:52,160
Post to the slash author's endpoint and send the data that you want to update in the body of your request.

380
00:27:52,366 --> 00:27:54,720
Now these fields will be defined for you somewhere.

381
00:27:55,054 --> 00:27:56,000
Want to remove an author?

382
00:27:56,238 --> 00:27:58,852
Send a delete to that specific author resource.

383
00:27:59,438 --> 00:28:09,028
Updates typically support the patch verb, and you'll see we're able to represent our CRUD needs, the create, request, update, and delete using HTTP methods.

384
00:28:09,280 --> 00:28:10,116
Pretty cool, right?

385
00:28:10,606 --> 00:28:15,396
What this means is that you can interact with any application out there that's exposed their REST API.

386
00:28:15,694 --> 00:28:19,972
You could grab data, remotely automate tasks, and even smush together different services.

387
00:28:20,654 --> 00:28:21,920
This is called a mashup.

388
00:28:22,446 --> 00:28:28,080
And here's the thing: there are a ton of APIs available, just about anything that you can imagine.

